310:## Core requirements
311-
312-They serve the vision, `vision-statements/caches-memory-vision-statements.md`, and cover the two memory caches,
313-full-text and vector; the code graph is its own project (`docs/code-graph/`). They are grouped by what happens to a
314-cache: it is **built**, it is **kept** true, and it is **read** (EJ, 2026-10-12: *"let rewrite using these 3
315-categories first"*). Each requirement names its source: **(v)** is the vision, **(d)** is this document's text, and
316-*proposed* marks wording EJ has not decided. Each has a check, **Fails if**, that some case breaks. There are no fixed
317-numbers: where a limit applies, it is a comparison that measurement decides. The ids R1–R9 they replace are given in
318-brackets, because run records cite them.
319-
320-### Build — when a cache comes to exist, and what goes in it
321-
322-**B1 — Nothing is built before its trigger fires** (v: never built before its own trigger; was R5)
323-Each cache has an observable trigger. This is the requirement that keeps the whole layer honest. The query-time
324-counterpart — which store to reach for once a trigger has fired — is the section **When to use which store** above.
325-
326-- **B1.0 EJ decides** — when a cache's comparison passes, EJ decides whether it is built; a passed comparison alone
327-  builds nothing (EJ, 2026-10-12: *"EJ decides"*; the 5W1H tables below say the same). The agent that told EJ
328-  writes down his decision — build or not, his words, the date — beside the comparison result in `runs/<run-id>/`
329-  (*proposed*; EJ, 2026-10-12: *"yes, write them in"*).
330-- **B1.1 Full-text** — built only when, on a question set built for the comparison (B1.3), full-text reaches the right
331-  entry after reading less than `INDEX.md` plus grep does, with no more misses. Measured as the code-and-docs benchmark
332-  was (BM-1 to BM-9): the same questions through both routes, the characters read to the first right entry, and the
333-  misses. Characters read is a proxy, not a token count, and a weak one: it stops at the right file, not the right
334-  answer (**Measured here**, who wrote each check). A route answers a question when the right entry is in its top 5
335-  results; a question it does not answer counts as a miss, never left out as the benchmark did (*"A miss is left out,
336-  never scored 0"*, `bench/results.md`); characters are compared only over the questions both routes answered
337-  (*proposed*). The benchmark had no `INDEX.md` arm; this comparison adds one. *Not decided:* how the
338-  `INDEX.md`-plus-grep route orders its results, and so what its top 5 is (the benchmark's `rg` ranked by distinct
339-  terms matched, then matches per file, `bench/README.md`). The comparison decides; no fixed size is
340-  set in advance (S-F5: nothing is measured yet for a corpus of a few hundred to a few thousand short entries; S-F13
341-  measured only speed, on synthetic entries). (was R5.1)
342-  - **The comparison** runs on a trial index built in `runs/<run-id>/`, never in `project-memory/.cache/`
343-    (*proposed*). It runs when EJ asks; the agent that runs it writes the result there; if it passes, the agent tells
344-    EJ, who decides (B1.0) (*proposed*; EJ, 2026-10-13: *"remove this idea , sounds very weird"*).
345-- **B1.2 Vector** — built only when, on a question set built for the comparison (B1.3), the vector index (the hybrid
346-  index, under the proposal below) finds an entry that full-text missed, and loses nothing full-text already found. (was
347-  R5.2; EJ, 2026-10-12: *"ok write it into CACHES.md"*)
348-  - **The question set** includes questions written in words that share no term with their entry, the kind
349-    `INDEX.md`, grep and full-text all miss (V-F13, the researcher's inference; BM-2 tested such questions)
350-    (*proposed*; EJ, 2026-10-13: *"remove this idea , sounds very weird"*).
351-  - **Finds** means the right entry is in the top 5 results (*proposed*; the benchmark's measure, BM-1 to BM-5).
352-  - **Loses nothing** means: every question in the set that full-text answers is still answered, and the
353-    characters read to the first right entry, over the questions both answered, are no more than full-text's
354-    (*proposed*; the same measure as B1.1, with its weakness).
355-  - **The comparison** runs on a trial index built in `runs/<run-id>/`, never in `project-memory/.cache/`
356-    (*proposed*). It runs when EJ asks; the agent that runs it writes the result there; if it passes, the agent tells
357-    EJ, who decides (B1.0) (*proposed*).
358-  - *Proposed* (hybrid only, **Vector index** why 5; EJ, 2026-10-12: *"label hybrid as proposed"*): the vector index
359-    is combined with full-text, so it is built only after B1.1 has fired, and the comparison runs on the hybrid
360-    index.
361-  - **Fails if** a vector index exists in `project-memory/.cache/` with no recorded comparison in which it found at
362-    least one entry full-text missed and lost none of full-text's answers, or with no recorded decision by EJ.
363-- **B1.3 The question set** for a comparison is built when the comparison runs: questions with known answers, written
364-  from the entries that exist then and checked by a second agent, as the code-and-docs benchmark was (**Measured
365-  here**: codex wrote the questions, five Claude agents checked them). The run records who wrote and who checked it
366-  (*proposed*; moved from Rd4.2, EJ, 2026-10-13: *"remove this idea , sounds very weird"*).
367-- **Fails if** a cache exists under `project-memory/.cache/` with no recorded comparison showing its trigger fired,
368-  or with no recorded decision by EJ to build it; a passed comparison has no record that EJ was told; or a comparison
369-  ran on a question set with no record of who wrote it and who checked it.
370-
371-**B2 — Derived from the files** (v: never the source of truth; was R1)
372-The cache is computed from the memory's markdown and never holds knowledge of its own (M4, M6; M6 is marked
373-CONTESTED in its source, where one engine proposed SQLite as a structured state store).
374-
375-- **B2.1** Deleting the cache and rebuilding from the same files restores every answer, provided the vector index is
376-  rebuilt with the same embedding model and settings (a changed model makes every vector useless, V-F24).
377-  *Example:* `rm -rf project-memory/.cache/` → run the documented rebuild → the same query returns the same entries.
378-  (M6) (was R1.1)
379-- **B2.2** The cache directory is ignored by git (`git check-ignore` reports it), and a rebuild adds nothing to
380-  `git status --short` that was not there before it. (M6) (was R1.3)
381-- **Fails if** the same query returns different entries after a delete and a rebuild from the same files with the
382-  same embedding model, `git check-ignore` does not report the cache directory, or a rebuild adds a line to
383-  `git status --short`.
384-
385-**B3 — Full rebuild, never patched** (v: never patched; was R2.1)
386-- **B3.1** The documented rebuild regenerates the whole index from the files; no incremental update path exists.
387-  *Example:* the rebuild command has no "update" mode. (G-F5 drift)
388-- **Fails if** the rebuild has an update or incremental mode, or reuses any row or vector from the previous index.
389-
390-**B4 — Only active entries** (d: Rules for every cache, my reading; was R6)
391-- **B4.1** The index holds every file in `entries/` whose own header says `status: active`, and nothing else — not
392-  `journal/` or `work/` — each row carrying `covers`. Every row is active, so no row stores `status`
393-  (*proposed*; EJ, 2026-10-12: *"others can accept and apply"*). The headers are the reference, not
394-  `INDEX.md`, which is generated from them (`SOURCE_OF_TRUTH.md` K6.1) (*proposed*). *Example:* a superseded entry
395-  never appears in a search result as if current. (V-F22, my reading)
396-- **Fails if** the index holds a file whose header is not `status: active`, lacks an active entry, holds a file
397-  outside `entries/`, or has a row without `covers`.
398-
399-**B5 — No model call to rebuild, except embedding** (v: How we know it works; was R8.1)
400-- **B5.1** The documented rebuild completes without a model call, except the embedding step of B1.2.
401-  *Example:* rebuild with no model credentials available; the full-text path still works. (M4, M6)
402-- **Fails if** the full-text rebuild calls a model, or the vector rebuild calls any model but its embedding model.
403-
404-### Keep — the cache stays true to the files, and stays worth having
405-
406-**K1 — It records what it was built from** (d: Rules for every cache; was R2.2)
407-- **K1.1** The index records a content hash of each entry file it was built from, so a stale one is detectable,
408-  including edits not yet committed (*proposed*: a commit alone misses those). A newest-mtime check, the one `S-F9`
409-  uses, is not enough: a deletion leaves no newer file (K2.1; my reading). (S-F11: SHA-256 on files)
410-- **K1.2** The vector index also records the id of the embedding model it was built with, because a changed model
411-  makes every vector useless (V-F24; the vector 5W1H stores it beside each vector).
412-- **Fails if** the index carries no content hash of its sources, or the vector index carries no embedding-model id.
413-
414-**K2 — Stale never answers** (d: Rules for every cache; was R3.1)
415-- **K2.1** Before answering, the tool compares the recorded content hash with the entry files, and for the vector
416-  index the recorded model id with the one a rebuild would use now (*proposed*). If either differs it rebuilds,
417-  building into a temporary file and renaming over the old one — so a deleted entry cannot stay searchable. (S-F9:
418-  rebuild before answering; S-F11: the hash)
419-- **K2.2** Only one rebuild runs at a time in a working copy: a second one waits for it, then compares the hash again
420-  (K2.1) before answering. Each worktree has its own `.cache/`, because the folder is gitignored, so agents on
421-  different worktrees never share one (*proposed*; EJ, 2026-10-12: *"yes, write them in"*).
422-- **K2.3** When both indexes are built, they are rebuilt by one command, in one step. If either fails, neither
423-  replaces the old ones, and the query falls back (Rd3.5) (*proposed*; the vector 5W1H and V-F24: a second rebuild
424-  step lets the two disagree).
425-- **Fails if** after an entry is added, changed or deleted, or the embedding model changes, a query answers from the
426-  old index; two rebuilds write to one cache at once; or the two indexes were built from different content hashes.
427-
428-**K3 — Dropped when it stops paying** (d; vision: *"cheap … and not before"*; was R8.2; EJ, 2026-10-12: *"yes please
429-write"*)
430-- **K3.1** A built cache is expected to keep passing its own trigger (K3.3 says what happens when it does not): its B1
431-  comparison is rerun, on a question set built then (B1.3) (*proposed*;
432-  the rebuild-cost part was dropped, EJ, 2026-10-12: *"drop the K3 cost part"*).
433-- **K3.2** The comparison is rerun when EJ asks; the agent that runs it writes the result to `runs/<run-id>/`
434-  (*proposed*, the same as B1.1 and B1.2).
435-- **K3.3** If the cache no longer passes, the agent tells EJ, who decides whether to drop it; his decision is recorded
436-  as in B1.0. Dropping is deleting it; memory is unaffected (B2, Rd3) (*proposed*, mirroring B1.0).
437-- **Fails if** a cache stays in `project-memory/.cache/` after a rerun it failed, with no recorded decision by EJ to
438-  keep it.
439-
440-### Read — an agent queries it
441-
442-**Rd1 — Never read at session start** (v: never read at session start; was R9)
443-- **Rd1.1** No cache file is part of what a session loads at start. *Example:* the session-start reads in
444-  `SOURCE_OF_TRUTH.md` (§ When it will be used) name no file under `project-memory/.cache/`; a start hook that
445-  opens `.cache/index.sqlite` fails this. (M18; CP6, codex: *"Do not bulk-load … an entire search index at
446-  startup"*)
447-- **Fails if** a start hook or instruction opens any file under `project-memory/.cache/`.
448-
449-**Rd2 — An answer names its entry** (d; was R7)
450-- **Rd2.1** Every result prints the entry file it came from, so the agent can open the source before acting on it
451-  (M19: *"open the primary source when a retrieved statement will determine an action"*). *Example:* search output
452-  names `entries/D-0012-*.md`. (*proposed*: "the agent", not "a human", per M19 and the vision; EJ, 2026-10-12:
453-  *"write fixes 1–4 into CACHES.md"*)
454-- **Fails if** a result does not name its entry file.
455-
456-**Rd3 — Absence degrades, it does not fail** (v: delete the folder and memory is unaffected; was R1.2, R3.2, R4)
457-- **Rd3.1** Memory is fully readable with no cache present. *Example:* with `.cache/` absent, session start and an
458-  on-demand `grep` over `entries/` both work. (M6) (was R1.2)
459-- **Rd3.2** A missing cache file or a missing FTS5 falls back to grep, and every fallback answer says it came from
460-  grep, not the cache, so the agent knows its results are unranked. (S-F9) (was R4.1; *proposed*: "says so in every
461-  answer" replaces "warns once")
462-- **Rd3.3** A missing embedding model falls back to full-text if it is there, and to grep if not; the answer says
463-  so. *(my reading, extending S-F9; *proposed*: full-text before grep.)* (was R4.2)
464-- **Rd3.4** A wrong path is never mistaken for an empty index: the cache is opened read-only and "no such file" is
465-  treated as absent. *Example:* point the tool at a typo'd path → it falls back to grep and says so, it does not
466-  create an empty database. (G-F5 drift, failure mode 5) (was R3.2)
467-- **Rd3.5** A rebuild that fails falls back to grep; it never answers from the stale index (K2) (*proposed*).
468-- **Fails if** with the cache, FTS5 or the embedding model missing, the path mistyped, or a rebuild failing, a query
469-  errors, answers from an empty or stale index, or answers from grep without saying so.
