== W1.1a MUST |check: tool; git |who: none
fails_if: a file's prefix is not one of the five or does not match its `kind`, `kind` holds any other value, or git shows an entry file renamed to a different prefix.
text: Each entry is one file. The prefix is one letter per kind (W2): `D-` decision, `F-` fact, `L-` lesson, `O-` observation and `H-` hypothesis. It matches the entry's `kind`. There is no runbook prefix (W3.6). An entry's kind, and so its prefix, never changes. A changed kind (a hypothesis confirmed, an observation that turns out to be a fact) is a new entry that supersedes the old one (K4.1).

== W1.1b MUST |check: tool; git |who: none
fails_if: an id is not in the W1.1 form, two entries share an id, or git shows an entry file renamed to a different id.
text: The `id` is `DD-MM-xxxxxx-YYYY`: the day, month and year the entry is written, six random lowercase letters and digits, for example `13-10-k3f7a2-2026`. Writers on different branches pick ids without coordinating, so an id is never "the next free number". No two entries share an id. An id never changes, so the file keeps its name.

== W1.2a MUST |check: tool |who: none
fails_if: a header is not between two `---` lines.
text: The header starts on the file's first line, which is exactly `---`, and ends at the next line that is exactly `---`.

== W1.3a MUST |check: tool; reviewer |who: none
fails_if: an active entry has no claim or no `**Why:**` line, or a reviewer finds two claims in one entry.
text: The body follows the header: the claim, then a line starting with `**Why:**`. There is one claim per entry.

== W4.1b MUST |check: tool |who: none
fails_if: an entry's `status` is any other value.
text: `status` is only `active`, `superseded` or `retired`.

== W4.2 SHOULD |check: git |who: none
fails_if: git shows a change to an entry with no matching `changed:` line, or an earlier line edited, with no written reason.
text: Every change to an entry adds one line to its `changed:` log: the time, and who made it (the model ID for an agent, the name for a person). Earlier lines are never edited.

== W6.1a MUST |check: reviewer; git |who: none
fails_if: an entry file holds more than one entry, or a commit adds an entry to an existing file.
text: Each new entry gets its own file. Agents never append to one shared file.

== W6.3a MUST |check: git; reviewer |who: project owner
fails_if: a merged entry is missing a `changed:` line, link or `covers` path that one side added, or two entries superseding one go unreported.
text: A claim is never rewritten (K4.3), so a merge conflict in an entry is never in its claim: only in its header (`covers`, `status`, the links) and the lines added to it. Whoever does the merge keeps both sides: both `changed:` lines, both links, and every `covers` path either side wrote. Nobody chooses between two claims. Two entries that each supersede the same entry are reported in the task's `state.md` and to the project owner, as in Rd5.3.

== K4.1 MUST |check: git; reviewer |who: none
fails_if: git shows an entry file deleted, a `superseded` entry with no `superseded_by` link, or a `retired` entry with no reason in its `changed:` line.
text: An entry is never deleted. A `superseded` entry links to what replaced it (`superseded_by`). A `retired` entry, which nothing replaces, gives its reason in its `changed:` line. Either keeps its old body.

== K4.3 MUST |check: git |who: none
fails_if: git shows an entry's claim, `source` or `depends_on` changed after its first commit, or its `covers` changed with no `changed:` line.
text: An entry's claim is never rewritten. A changed claim is a new entry that supersedes the old one. After an entry is first committed, it only gains a `changed:` line, a new `status`, the `superseded_by` link, and `done:` on a decision to build something (W5.3). Its `covers` may also be corrected, for example after a file is renamed, with a `changed:` line; `source` and `depends_on` change only by superseding the entry. A `covers` correction changes where the entry points, not what it claims, so it is not a mismatch under Rd5.3. This also keeps merge conflicts out of claims (W6.3).

== K5.1 SHOULD |check: git |who: none
fails_if: a journal file is edited after it is written, or raw notes are kept elsewhere, with no written reason.
text: Task and project memory each keep a journal: a task's in `work/<task-id>/journal/`, the project's in `journal/` for work not tied to a task. Both hold raw notes, one file per agent session, appended to and never edited. Agent and user memory keep no journal under this design.

== K6.1a MUST |check: tool |who: none
fails_if: a commit's `INDEX.md` differs from what the tool builds from that commit's entries, or lists a superseded or retired entry as active.
text: `INDEX.md` is generated from `entries/`: one line per active entry, showing its id, kind and one-line claim.

== K6.2 MUST |check: tool |who: none
fails_if: a commit's `INDEX.md` differs from what the tool builds from that commit's entries.
text: Whenever an entry is created or changed (any kind, any status change), `INDEX.md` is regenerated and committed in the same commit as the entry, so the next session, on any clone, sees it. `tools/memory_index.py` builds it; its `--check [<rev>]` compares the `INDEX.md` in the staging area, or in a commit, with what it builds from the entries there. Its requirements and design are in `docs/index-md/`.

== K6.3a MUST |check: tool |who: none
fails_if: an entry holds a git conflict-marker line (`<<<<<<<` or `>>>>>>>`). In `INDEX.md`, the comparison in K6.2 catches it.
text: When a merge conflicts, resolve the entry files first, with no conflict markers left in them.

== Rd3.1a MUST |check: tool |who: project owner: which agents work on the project, and so the strictest limit
fails_if: `INDEX.md` exceeds that strictest limit (until it is recorded, the interim numbers), or the K6 check passes an `INDEX.md` that exceeds it.
text: `INDEX.md` fits the strictest start-file limit among the agents that work on the project. The check that keeps it in step with its entries (K6) also fails when it does not fit. Until the project owner records measured limits, the tool's limit is the example in Rd3.1b: 200 lines and 24,000 bytes.
